/** The text of errors.md before its first entry. */
export const ERROR_INTRO: string = "# Portaldot V3: errors and traps\n\nWhat developers meet on Portaldot V3, why it happens, and what to do about it. Written by the Bridge Zero team and checked against the live chain on Nov 14 and 15, 2026, where an entry says so. An error text given with a measurement date is an exact copy of what the chain returned; standard Substrate texts that we did not measure are marked as such.\n\nEach entry has up to six parts, always in this order: **You see** (the error text or the symptom), **Cause**, **Fix**, **Not known**, **Others report** (what other builders say; names are left out) and **Basis**. The Basis line says how we know, with one of four words:\n\n- **Checked**: measured on the chain by the Bridge Zero team, on the date given.\n- **Experience**: learned while building and running our own tools on this chain. Not measured again.\n- **Guide**: from the chain team's testnet guide.\n- **Reported**: said by other builders. We did not check it.\n\nE1 to E7 are the ones met most often.\n\nRecipe numbers refer to two files. In references/recipes.md: 0 connect and check the chain; 1 send the native token; 5 read a transaction's result and events on both sides (5a an Ethereum transaction, 5b a native extrinsic, 5c contract events from a native-path call, 5d in a browser); 6 convert between the two address forms; 7 an account's balance in both forms; 8 estimate a fee; 9 nonces, finality and sending safely. In references/recipes-contracts.md: 2 Solidity with Foundry and cast (2a build, 2b read, 2c call by Ethereum transaction, 2d deploy by Ethereum transaction, 2e deploy on the native path, 2f read why the node refuses an Ethereum transaction, without sending it); 3 call a contract from a native account; 4 call a native module from a 0x account.\n\nThe chain tool explain_error matches a pasted error text against these entries.";

export const ERROR_ENTRIES: ErrorEntry[] = [
  { id: "E1", title: "Error 1010 \"Invalid Transaction\" when sending", body: "- **You see:** the node refuses the transaction when you submit it, and nothing is mined.\n  - From the Ethereum RPC (`eth_sendRawTransaction`, and so from MetaMask and `cast send`): `{\"code\":1010,\"message\":\"Invalid Transaction\"}`. It gives no reason. Recipe 2f reads the reason from the node without sending.\n  - From the Substrate RPC, the reason follows: `{\"code\":1010,\"message\":\"Invalid Transaction\",\"data\":\"Inability to pay some fees (e.g. account balance too low)\"}`. @polkadot/api 17 prints it as `1010: Invalid Transaction: Inability to pay some fees , e.g. account balance too low`, with a comma where the node's text has brackets (Checked on Nov 15, 2026).\n  - Before sending, `eth_estimateGas` and `eth_call` with a value the sender cannot pay answer `{\"code\":-32000,\"message\":\"insufficient funds for gas * price + value (0): address 0x... have 0 (supplied gas ...)\"}`. The `(0)` is not the amount needed: it was 0 for a 1 tPOT transfer too. `eth_call` ends the text with `(supplied gas None)`, `eth_estimateGas` with `(supplied gas Some(18446744073709551615))` (Checked on Nov 15, 2026).\n  - The same refused bytes sent again answer `{\"code\":1013,\"message\":\"Transaction Already Imported\"}` or `{\"code\":1012,\"message\":\"Transaction is temporarily banned\"}`, although nothing is pending (see Fix).\n- **Cause:** the transaction failed the node's checks before it ran.\n  - The common case for an Ethereum transaction: the sender's balance is smaller than the gas limit times the base fee (`eth_gasPrice`), plus the value. On V3 the gas includes the storage deposit (E2), so gas limits are very large. A padded limit, or one copied from another chain, can ask for more than the account holds even when the real cost is small.\n  - The Ethereum RPC gives the same text for other refusals too. A transaction with a max fee of 1 wei, below the base fee, got the same answer. It came from an empty account, so which check refused it is not known.\n  - Measured on Nov 15, 2026: the RPC decodes and passes on a transaction signed for another chain id (1), a blob transaction (type 3, sent without its sidecar) and a set-code transaction (type 4, EIP-7702). Each came back as the same bare 1010. The node's own check (recipe 2f) names `Call` for these three, and `Payment` for the balance cases. A legacy transaction without a chain id does not get this far: the RPC refuses it itself (E35).\n  - From a new, empty account, a gas limit of 21000, a max fee of half the gas price and a nonce of 5 also got the bare 1010, and the node's check said `Payment` for each. An empty account fails the balance check anyway, so what each of these mistakes does by itself is not measured.\n  - On the Substrate RPC, the text after \"Invalid Transaction\" names the check. Measured on this chain: \"Inability to pay some fees (e.g. account balance too low)\" (the account cannot pay the fee, or does not exist yet) and \"Transaction has a bad signature\" (the wrong key, a payload signed for another chain or runtime version, or a mortal transaction sent after its validity period). A standard Substrate text, not measured by us on this chain: \"Transaction is outdated\" (the nonce was already used, E12).\n  - Measured on Nov 15, 2026, from new, empty accounts: \"Transaction has an ancient birth block\" for a mortal transaction born 3000 blocks back with a period of 4096 (the chain keeps 2400 block hashes); \"Transaction call is not expected\" for an ordinary call sent unsigned (`System.remark`); and \"Transaction has a bad signature\" for each of these mistakes: signed for another genesis hash, another spec version or another transaction version; the call or one byte of the signature changed after signing; an sr25519 signature marked as ed25519; and a mortal transaction signed 200 blocks back with a period of 64. A nonce of 5 on an account at 0, and a tip of 10^30, still answered \"Inability to pay some fees\". Bytes the node cannot read as a transaction get 1040, 1001 or 1002 instead (E34), and an unsigned `Balances.transferKeepAlive` got 1011 (E12).\n- **Fix:**\n  - Gas limit = `eth_estimateGas` + 10%, not more. Max fee = `eth_gasPrice` + 1/8, with a priority fee of 0 (recipe 2c). The chain tool estimate_eth_transaction applies this rule and checks the balance.\n  - Before signing, check that the balance >= value + gas limit x max fee. That is stricter than the refusal we measured (which used the base fee), so a transaction that passes it is not refused for its balance while the base fee stays below the max fee; estimate_eth_transaction checks with the max fee for that reason.\n  - Fund a MetaMask account by sending native tPOT to its fallback `5...` address (recipe 6, E4).\n  - A refused transaction did not enter the pool and did not use its nonce. Fix the cause, then sign again. Sending the same bytes again soon after gives `{\"code\":1012,\"message\":\"Transaction is temporarily banned\"}` (measured for a native extrinsic).\n  - On Nov 15, 2026, Ethereum transactions refused with 1010 and sent again about 0.6 s later got `{\"code\":1013,\"message\":\"Transaction Already Imported\"}` in 7 of 9 cases and 1012 in the other 2; the same delay gave 1013 for one transaction and 1012 for another when we measured again. One transaction got 1013 twice in a row, then 1012 6 s later and 30 s after that. Nothing was pending at any time (E12), so neither code means that the transaction is waiting. A native extrinsic got 1012 each time.\n  - To learn which check refuses an Ethereum transaction, ask the node without sending it (recipe 2f in `recipes-contracts.md`). It answered `Invalid Payment` for an empty account and `Invalid Call` for a transaction signed for chain id 1.\n- **Others report:** that every write from an external Ethereum account was refused with \"Invalid Transaction\". That is the text of the balance check above, so check that rule first. Our own Ethereum transactions went through when they followed it.\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 14, 2026: the texts marked as measured come from transactions that a brand-new account with no balance sent on purpose, so that they could not be included (three Ethereum transactions and three native extrinsics), and from dry runs. The balance rule was Checked on Nov 13 and 14, 2026. The fix is Experience from eight transactions sent between Oct 19 and Nov 13, 2026. Checked on Nov 15, 2026, the same way: refused submissions from new, empty accounts with random keys held only in memory, to the Substrate node (`author_submitExtrinsic` and `author_submitAndWatchExtrinsic`, testnet blocks 475079 to 475097) and to `eth_sendRawTransaction` (blocks 474651 to 474794, part of them again at blocks 474908 to 474913). Before each native case we read the node's own check of the same bytes (`TaggedTransactionQueue_validate_transaction` through `state_call`). For the Ethereum transactions we read it for a second copy signed the same way by another empty key (the replies to both copies were the same), and, when we repeated the measurement, for the exact bytes sent. The `(0)` and the `supplied gas` texts come from dry runs of Nov 15, 2026. Nothing was accepted." },
  { id: "E2", title: "The gas estimate looks far too large (MetaMask shows a review alert)", body: "- **You see:** `eth_estimateGas`, a receipt's `gasUsed` or MetaMask show a gas amount that looks absurd for the work, and MetaMask may show a review alert. The gas used by the same call also changes from day to day.\n  - On Nov 14, 2026, at a gas price of 15800 wei: 778831307143 gas for a call that does nothing (0.0123 tPOT), and 7158578160885 gas for a 1 tPOT payment to a new account (0.1131 tPOT, which includes the new account's 0.1 tPOT existential deposit). At 15685 wei: 249985957328367 gas for an ERC-20 `approve` that writes one new slot (3.92 tPOT), and 5900202211390958 gas to deploy 1000 bytes of runtime code (92.54 tPOT).\n- **Cause:** the Ethereum side folds the storage deposit into gas, so gas x gas price is the fee plus the deposit.\n  - A new 32-byte contract storage slot holds 3.8415 tPOT. A new account holds 0.1 tPOT.\n  - Deploying new runtime code of L bytes holds 0.078 x L + 13.11 tPOT, before any storage the constructor writes: 13.188 tPOT for 1 byte, 83.232 tPOT for 899 bytes, 98.91 tPOT for 1100 bytes. When the exact same code is already stored on the chain, its own (L + 70) x 0.06 part is not charged again; the rest is.\n  - The gas price moves, so the same deposit is a different amount of gas. On the testnet it was 38840 wei on Oct 19, 2026 and about 15700 wei on Nov 14, 2026 (it fell from 15825 to 15575 during that day); on the mainnet it was 5000 wei, read through the node on Nov 14, 2026. Two identical contract calls, each costing 3.864 tPOT (3.8415 of it deposit), used 103.97 x 10^12 gas at 37165 wei and 153.92 x 10^12 gas at 25105 wei.\n- **Fix:**\n  - Convert to tokens before judging: gas x gas price / 10^18 = tPOT.\n  - Compare with the deposit you expect: new slots x 3.8415, plus the code, plus 0.1 for each new account.\n  - The native path reports a small fee and a separate deposit for the same work. A contract call that wrote one new slot paid a fee of 0.0253 tPOT and a deposit of 3.8415 tPOT.\n  - Never hard-code the gas price or a gas limit. A limit taken from Ethereum habits is far too small (E27).\n- **Others report:** a gas estimate that differed from the real cost by a factor of 8258, and MetaMask alerts on ordinary transactions.\n- **Basis:** Checked: the estimates, the gas prices and the deploy deposits on Nov 14, 2026 (`eth_estimateGas`, dry runs, and the mainnet node's `reviveApi.gasPrice`); the slot deposit and the gas used from dry runs and transactions between Oct 19 and Nov 1, 2026. The fix is Experience." },
  { id: "E3", title: "Amounts off by 10000 (14 against 18 decimals)", body: "- **You see:** a payment 10000 times too large or too small, a contract that refuses a value, or balances that disagree between Polkadot tools and MetaMask.\n- **Cause:** native amounts have 14 decimals. The Ethereum side shows the same token with 18 (`nativeToEthRatio` is 10000).\n  - The `value` of `revive.call` is in native units, while `msg.value`, an Ethereum transaction's `value` and `eth_getBalance` are in wei.\n  - `eth_getBalance` also leaves out the 0.1 tPOT existential deposit, or the frozen amount when that is larger (E17).\n- **Fix:**\n  - wei = native units x 10^4. Keep Ethereum values a multiple of 10^4 wei. Some contracts refuse other values; that is a rule of the contract, not of the chain.\n  - Read the ratio live (`api.consts.revive.nativeToEthRatio`) rather than assuming it.\n  - Use BigInt. A JavaScript Number is exact only up to 2^53, which is about 90 tPOT in 14-decimal units, or 0.009 tPOT in 18-decimal units.\n  - The chain tool convert_amount does the conversion.\n- **Not known:** what a sent transaction does with a value that is not a multiple of 10^4 wei. On Nov 14, 2026, `eth_call` and `eth_estimateGas` with a value of 1 wei were accepted without an error. The chain keeps a remainder below 10^4 wei as \"dust\" on each account (9271 wei seen on one account on Nov 14, 2026), and the error `Revive.BalanceConversionFailed` exists. We have not sent such a transaction.\n- **Others report:** seven forum topics about this, among them ethers' `parseEther(\"1.0\")` used for a native amount, which pays 10000 times too much with no error.\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 13 and 14, 2026 (the decimals, the ratio, the 1 wei dry runs). The fix is Experience." },
  { id: "E4", title: "Funds sent to the wrong form of an address", body: "- **You see:** tPOT left one wallet and never shows in the other; a MetaMask user who was paid sees nothing; tokens sent on another EVM chain cannot be moved.\n- **Cause:** every account has two forms: a native `5...` address (32 bytes) and a `0x` address (20 bytes). They are linked by a rule, not by a shared key.\n  - Native to `0x`: the last 20 bytes of keccak-256 of the 32-byte account id.\n  - `0x` to native: the native account linked to it (`revive.originalAccount(0x...)`), or, when none is linked (an Ethereum key such as MetaMask, or a contract), its fallback account: the 20 bytes followed by twelve `0xEE` bytes.\n  - A native account is linked when it is created (autoMap, E8). A native account that does not exist yet has no link, so tPOT sent from the Ethereum side to its `0x` address lands on a fallback account that no Ethereum key controls.\n  - A native account's `0x` address has no private key behind it (it is a hash of the account id). Tokens sent to it on another EVM chain can never move.\n  - tPOT sent to a contract with a plain transfer (`balances.transfer...` to its account) arrives, but the contract's code does not run, so its own bookkeeping never counts it.\n- **Fix:**\n  - To pay a MetaMask user from a native wallet, send to the fallback `5...` address of their `0x` address (recipe 6, or the chain tool convert_address). Sending to any other `5...` address does not reach them. The guide does the same with Developer > Runtime calls > reviveApi > accountId.\n  - To pay a native account from the Ethereum side, first check that `revive.originalAccount(0x...)` names it, or fund the account natively first.\n  - Give other EVM chains an address from an Ethereum key, never a native account's `0x` form.\n  - Pay a contract through its own functions.\n  - A faucet or form that takes only `5...` addresses: give it the fallback `5...` address of your `0x` address.\n  - The guide says: \"The H160 derived from the same private key/mnemonic is NOT the same as the address generated on Ethereum/BSC\". That holds for sr25519 keys, the default of Polkadot-style wallets. A MetaMask (secp256k1) key has the same `0x` address on V3 as on Ethereum.\n- **Not known:** whether `revive.dispatchAsFallbackAccount(call)` recovers funds in practice. It exists on the chain, and its text says every account id can control its fallback account as a recovery function. We have not tried it.\n- **Others report:** that this is the most common support question of the Portaldot community, and that a faucet paid only `5...` addresses while the wallet showed `0x`.\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 13 and 14, 2026: the rule against the chain's own `reviveApi.address` and `reviveApi.accountId`, and a MetaMask address whose tPOT sat on its fallback account. The guide's lines are Guide. The fixes are Experience." },
  { id: "E5", title: "State queries on old blocks fail (\"State already discarded\")", body: "- **You see:** `eth_getBalance`, `eth_getStorageAt` or `eth_call` at an older block answer \"State already discarded\", or `eth_getBlockByNumber` does not return an old block. A block after the head answers `{\"code\":-32000,\"message\":\"hash not found\"}` from `eth_getBalance` and `eth_call`, while `eth_getBlockByNumber` answers `null` for it. A block hash the RPC does not know answers `{\"code\":-32000,\"message\":\"Ethereum block not found\"}` from `eth_getBalance` and `eth_call` (E37), and `null` from `eth_getBlockByHash`.\n- **Cause:** in October 2026 the Ethereum RPC kept state for only about the last 256 blocks. Since Nov 13, 2026 it has served much older state: on Nov 14, 2026, `eth_getBalance` answered at block 1 and 300000 blocks back, and `eth_getBlockByNumber` returned block 1. The behaviour has changed once, so do not rely on either.\n- **Fix:**\n  - Read old state from the Substrate node, which served block 1 on Nov 13 and 14, 2026: `(await api.at(blockHash)).query...`.\n  - Or test the depth at run time before you depend on it.\n- **Others report:** an event that could no longer be found once its history became unavailable.\n- **Basis:** Checked in October 2026 (the 256-block limit, Oct 17 to Oct 20) and on Nov 13 and 14, 2026 (older state served, and the \"hash not found\" text). The replies for a block 100000 after the head and for a random block hash Checked on Nov 15, 2026, each measured twice. The fix is Experience." },
  { id: "E6", title: "Logs missing for native-path calls (eth_getLogs returns [])", body: "- **You see:** a contract emitted an event, but `eth_getLogs` and the receipt show nothing.\n- **Cause:** the Ethereum RPC indexes the logs of Ethereum transactions only. When a contract runs from a native extrinsic (`revive.call`, a multisig that dispatches it, or anything not sent through the Ethereum RPC), its events exist only as `revive.ContractEmitted { contract, data, topics }` in `System.Events`. The events of an Ethereum transaction are in both places, possibly in a different order.\n  - A native call sent from a `0x` account to the pallets address `0x6d6f646c70792f70616464720000000000000000` has a receipt with `gasUsed 0` and no logs. Its effects are in the Substrate events only.\n  - A large block range is not the reason: `eth_getLogs` has no block-range limit. From block 0 to the head it answered in under half a second for one address, and with no filter at all it returned 8004 logs in 24 s. A `fromBlock` after the `toBlock` is refused (E33).\n- **Fix:** read `System.Events` at the block and treat each `ContractEmitted` as a log: address = `contract`, plus `topics` and `data`. Decode it with the contract's ABI (recipe 5c, or the chain tool get_contract_events). To find the block without an index, binary-search old state, for example for the block where a counter in the contract's storage changed.\n- **Others report:** two other teams met the same thing.\n- **Basis:** Checked on Oct 20 and Oct 27, 2026 (native-path contract calls whose events `eth_getLogs` did not return), on Nov 13, 2026 (the pallets-address receipt) and on Nov 14, 2026 (testnet blocks 389240 and 463584: the node's events against `eth_getLogs`; the range tests, made twice). The fix is Experience." },
  { id: "E7", title: "The storage deposit limit is too low, or the deposit cannot be paid", body: "- **You see:** a `revive.call` or `revive.instantiateWithCode` fails as a whole with `Revive.StorageDepositLimitExhausted` (module 80, error 24, `{\"module\":{\"index\":80,\"error\":\"0x18000000\"}}`) or `Revive.StorageDepositNotEnoughFunds` (module 80, error 23, `\"0x17000000\"`).\n- **Cause:**\n  - `StorageDepositLimitExhausted` (\"More storage was created than allowed by the storage deposit limit\"): the limit you passed is below what the call holds at its peak (`maxStorageDeposit`), even when the net charge is 0. A dry run of a deploy with a limit of 1 base unit returned it.\n  - `StorageDepositNotEnoughFunds` (\"Origin doesn't have enough balance to pay the required storage deposits\"): the account cannot pay the deposit. A dry run of the same deploy from an account with no balance returned it.\n  - In a multisig, the approval round then has to be done again.\n- **Fix:**\n  - Dry-run first (`reviveApi.call` or `reviveApi.instantiate`, or the chain tools simulate_contract_call and simulate_contract_deploy).\n  - Set the limit to the larger of `storageDeposit` and `maxStorageDeposit`, plus 2%.\n  - Check that free >= value + limit + fee + 0.1 tPOT.\n  - If the call may create the payee's account, add 0.1 tPOT for its existential deposit. A dry run of a 1 tPOT payment to a new `0x` address reported a storage deposit of 0.1 tPOT.\n- **Others report:** that Revive dry runs need a storage deposit limit above zero. A dry run with no limit at all (`null` for `storage_deposit_limit`) works, and that is the simplest way to see the full deposit. A limit of 0 passed for a call that stores nothing (`decimals()`), and gave `StorageDepositLimitExhausted` for one that writes a new slot (`approve`).\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 14, 2026 (both errors and the 0.1 tPOT, by dry run; dry runs with `null` and with 0). The fix is Experience from contract calls and multisig rounds in October 2026." },
  { id: "E8", title: "Advice to \"call mapAccount first\" (AccountUnmapped)", body: "- **You see:** guides or samples that tell you to link a native account with `revive.mapAccount` before it can use contracts, or the error name `Revive.AccountUnmapped`.\n- **Cause:** upstream defaults. In the upstream sample runtime, automatic linking is off.\n- **Fix:** skip the step. On V3 `autoMap` is true on both networks: every native account is linked to its `0x` address when it is created. The chain's own text for `mapAccount` says \"Noop when `Config::AutoMap` is enabled\", and the chain also has the error `Revive.AutoMappingEnabled` (\"Manual mapping is disabled when auto-mapping is enabled\"), so calling it gains nothing.\n- **Not known:** whether `AccountUnmapped` can still fire on V3. A dry run of a contract call from an account that does not exist yet did not return it.\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 13 and 14, 2026 (`autoMap` on both networks; all native accounts linked on Nov 13; the texts and the dry run on Nov 14)." },
  { id: "E9", title: "A reverted Solidity call shows \"Success\" on the Substrate side", body: "- **You see:** the explorer or `ExtrinsicSuccess` says the `revive.ethTransact` extrinsic succeeded, yet the contract call reverted.\n- **Cause:** a revert is a result of the contract, not a failure of the extrinsic. The chain's text for `Revive.ContractReverted` says the error \"is only returned from extrinsics. When called directly or via RPC an `Ok` will be returned\", and the caller must read the flags.\n- **Fix:**\n  - On the Ethereum side, read the receipt's `status` (`0x1` success, `0x0` reverted).\n  - On the native side, look for `revive.EthExtrinsicRevert` among the extrinsic's events.\n  - In a dry run (`reviveApi.call` or `reviveApi.instantiate`), `flags.bits & 1` = 1 means it reverted, and `data` holds the revert data (E26).\n  - The same pattern on multisig: `multisig.asMulti` succeeds even when the inner call fails. Read the result inside `MultisigExecuted`.\n  - A reverted deployment still has a `contractAddress` in its receipt, but no code is there. Check `status`, or `eth_getCode` at that address, before you use it.\n- **Others report:** 91 of 91 reverted transactions showed Success on the Substrate side.\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 14, 2026 (the chain's texts, the `EthExtrinsicRevert` event, and a dry run of reverting code that returned `flags.bits` 1; three reverted creation transactions in testnet blocks 343008 to 343010: receipt `status 0x0` with a `contractAddress`, `eth_getCode` `0x` there, and on the node `revive.EthExtrinsicRevert` with `Revive.ContractTrapped` next to `system.ExtrinsicSuccess`). The multisig part is Experience from Oct 20, 2026." },
  { id: "E10", title: "A cryptic module error such as { index: 6, error: 2 }", body: "- **You see:** `{\"module\":{\"index\":80,\"error\":\"0x18000000\"}}`, `{ Module: { index: '6', error: '0x02000000' } }`, or \"module 6 error 2\".\n- **Cause:** dispatch errors and dry-run errors come as a module index and an error code. The first byte of `error` is the error's index within the module; the other bytes are usually 0.\n- **Fix:**\n  - Paste it into the chain tool explain_error, or name it with `api.registry.findMetaError(dispatchError.asModule)`.\n  - For a dry-run JSON, use `api.registry.findMetaError(Uint8Array.of(index, firstByteOfError))`. The result gives `section`, `name` and `docs`.\n  - Examples from Nov 14, 2026: 6 and `0x02000000` is `Balances.InsufficientBalance`; 80 and `0x03000000` is `Revive.OutOfGas` (a weight limit that is too low); 80 and `0x04000000` is `Revive.TransferFailed` (a value larger than the balance); 80 and `0x17000000` is `Revive.StorageDepositNotEnoughFunds`; 80 and `0x18000000` is `Revive.StorageDepositLimitExhausted` (E7).\n  - `Revive.TransferFailed` came for a value above the balance of an account that holds tPOT. From an account with no balance, a `Revive.call` with a value of 1 tPOT answered `Revive.StorageDepositNotEnoughFunds` instead, and so did an `instantiateWithCode` with a value of 0: from such an account this error does not point to the value. Fund the account.\n  - An index belongs to one runtime version. For an old error, name it with the metadata of its block (`api.at(blockHash)`).\n- **Others report:** other builders found these hard to read too.\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 14, 2026 (the indices and names above, from the chain's metadata and from dry runs). The two `Revive.call` replies Checked on Nov 15, 2026 by dry run through the pallets address (E29), each measured twice. The fix is Experience." },
  { id: "E11", title: "@polkadot/api warns about unknown extensions, or decodes Revive results wrongly", body: "- **You see:** `REGISTRY: Unknown signed extensions AuthorizeCall, EthSetOrigin found, treating them as no-effect` when you connect with @polkadot/api.\n- **Cause:**\n  - V3 transactions carry 12 signed extensions, and @polkadot/api does not know two of them, `AuthorizeCall` and `EthSetOrigin`.\n  - The results of `reviveApi.call` and `reviveApi.instantiate` are newer than @polkadot/api's built-in types.\n  - A block that upgrades the runtime has its events encoded by the old runtime.\n- **Fix:**\n  - Pass `signedExtensions: { AuthorizeCall: { extrinsic: {}, payload: {} }, EthSetOrigin: { extrinsic: {}, payload: {} } }` to `ApiPromise.create` (recipe 0).\n  - Read Revive dry-run results with `.toJSON()` and pick the fields by hand: `weightRequired`, `storageDeposit`, `maxStorageDeposit`, `result.ok` or `result.err`.\n  - Decode a block's events with its parent's metadata. `api.at(hash)` does this.\n- **Not known:** whether signing works without the two extensions. A fee estimate (`paymentInfo`) worked without them on Nov 14, 2026. Our tools always pass them.\n- **Others report:** that some extrinsics paying their fee in another asset (with an `assetId`) cannot be decoded by polkadot.js Apps.\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 14, 2026 (the 12 extensions, the same on both networks; the warning text; the fee estimate). The fixes are Experience." },
  { id: "E12", title: "Nonce trouble: a stuck, pending or double send", body: "- **You see:** a transaction that never confirms, a second transaction refused while the first is pending, or the same transfer made twice. Standard Substrate texts for these cases, not measured by us on this chain: \"Priority is too low\" (code 1014: another transaction with the same nonce is already in the pool) and \"Transaction is outdated\" (code 1010: the nonce was already used). Measured on Nov 15, 2026: `{\"code\":1013,\"message\":\"Transaction Already Imported\"}` (in @polkadot/api, `1013: Transaction Already Imported: Any { .. }`) and `1011: Unknown Transaction Validity: NoUnsignedValidator`.\n- **Cause:** a transaction still pending in the pool, or a retry after a crash. Both sides share one nonce per account: `eth_getTransactionCount` equals `System.Account.nonce`. So an Ethereum transaction and a native extrinsic from the same account take numbers from the same sequence.\n  - Code 1013 does not prove that a transaction is waiting. Refused bytes sent again within about 2 s got it: 7 of 9 Ethereum transactions resent 0.6 s after their 1010 (E1), and on the Substrate node the bytes `0x00`, which are no transaction at all, at the fifth send, after three 1012 replies (E34). The Ethereum transactions were not pending: `eth_getTransactionCount(from, \"pending\")` stayed `0x0`, and none of them was in the node's `author_pendingExtrinsics`.\n  - Code 1011 with `NoUnsignedValidator` came for a `Balances.transferKeepAlive` sent unsigned: the runtime has no check that takes this call unsigned. Sign it. An unsigned `System.remark` got 1010 \"Transaction call is not expected\" instead (E1).\n- **Fix:**\n  - Send only when there is nothing pending: `eth_getTransactionCount(from, \"latest\")` equals the `\"pending\"` count, or `system.accountNextIndex` equals `System.Account.nonce`.\n  - Sign with an explicit nonce.\n  - Write the transaction hash down before you submit it.\n  - After a restart, settle what is open before sending again (recipe 9).\n  - Before you wait on a 1013, check whether the transaction is pending (the `\"pending\"` count, or `author_pendingExtrinsics`). If it is not, compare the nonce it was signed with against the account's nonce in blocks (`eth_getTransactionCount(from, \"latest\")`, or `System.Account.nonce`). Still at it: the transaction is not in a block. It was refused or dropped, since a refused transaction does not use its nonce (E1). Find out why (E1, recipe 2f) and sign again with that same nonce, so that at most one of the two can be included. Past it: this transaction, or another with that nonce, was included. Look it up by its hash (recipe 5a, or 5b for a native extrinsic; recipe 9 step 3 finds the block) and do not send it again. We have not measured what a resend of an included transaction answers.\n- **Not known:** the Ethereum RPC's exact texts for a nonce that is too low or too high. On Nov 15, 2026 a nonce of 5 on an account at 0, from a new, empty account, got the bare 1010 from the Ethereum RPC (the node's check said `Payment`) and \"Inability to pay some fees\" from the Substrate node. An empty account fails the balance check first, so this does not show what a funded account gets for a nonce too high. A nonce that is too low cannot be made from an empty account.\n- **Others report:** a reorganisation made a settled transfer look failed, the script retried, and tPOT moved twice. Wait for finality (E14).\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 14, 2026 (the shared nonce). That the two sides compete for one sequence follows from it; we never sent both at once. The 1013 and 1011 replies, the nonce of 5 and the pending checks after them Checked on Nov 15, 2026, from refused submissions of new, empty accounts on both RPCs. The fix is Experience." },
  { id: "E13", title: "The wrong chain, a reset testnet, or the guide's old endpoints", body: "- **You see:** errors from `*.feso-apps.xyz`, a node that answers with another genesis hash, or `{\"code\":-32601,\"message\":\"Method not found\"}` for `eth_chainId`.\n- **Cause:**\n  - The guide lists the October endpoints `wss://testnetv3-node.feso-apps.xyz` and `https://testnetv3-eth-rpc.feso-apps.xyz`. On Oct 10, 2026 they answered HTTP 502; on Nov 14, 2026 these host names no longer existed in public DNS (\"Non-existent domain\", and in Node `getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND`). The live endpoints are `wss://test-node-v3.portaldot.dev` (Substrate) and `https://test-evm-rpc-v3.portaldot.dev` (Ethereum).\n  - The Substrate nodes do not answer `eth_` methods: both `https://test-node-v3.portaldot.dev` and `https://live-v3-node.portaldot.dev` answer `eth_chainId` with \"Method not found\". Ethereum tools need the separate Ethereum RPC.\n  - The mainnet's Ethereum chain id is 780780777, but it has no public Ethereum RPC, so MetaMask and other Ethereum tools cannot use the mainnet.\n- **Fix:** before any send, check the genesis hash (testnet `0xd4f9acbcf7f52388d8b095abe3c198d19fc510e0343e643e87cfbccb155283bd`, mainnet `0x6e7f1f24628d2fa3a1ac81a8b45157c63b1037fee4123a7664aae2c55cdbbc36`), `revive.chainId` and `eth_chainId` (testnet 420420777, `0x190f1ca9`). Refuse on a mismatch: it is another chain or a reset testnet (recipe 0).\n- **Basis:** Guide (the old endpoints). Checked on Oct 10, 2026 (HTTP 502), on Nov 13, 2026 (no mainnet Ethereum RPC) and on Nov 14, 2026 (the DNS answers, \"Method not found\", the genesis hashes and chain ids). The fix is Experience." },
  { id: "E14", title: "The chain stops, or the RPC fails (halts, 502, 503, 429, timeouts)", body: "- **You see:** requests hang, the head stops moving, the WebSocket closes with code 1006, or errors 502, 503 and 429 come back.\n- **Cause:**\n  - One operator runs all public endpoints, and we know of no status page.\n  - The testnet has 2 validators and has stopped twice: from Oct 28, 2026, 12:45 PM UTC to Oct 29, 2:09 AM UTC (13 h 24 min), and on Oct 31, 2026 from 10:58 AM to 4:14 PM UTC (5 h 16 min).\n- **Fix:**\n  - Use timeouts and retries. What has worked for us: reconnect the WebSocket after 2.5 s, time a request out after 60 s, and retry an HTTP request 3 times, 2 s apart.\n  - Check that the head is moving before you look for a fault in your code (the chain tool get_chain_facts shows it).\n  - Treat a transfer as done only once its block is finalized. Wait up to 10 minutes for that before you treat it as pending.\n- **Others report:** 503 and 429 answers, and refused log ranges. `eth_getLogs` refused no range in our tests of Nov 14, 2026, from block 0 to the head included (E6).\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 13, 2026 (one operator; we found no status page). The two stops were Checked from block timestamps on Nov 14, 2026. The fix is Experience." },
  { id: "E15", title: "A receipt that is missing for days", body: "- **You see:** `eth_getTransactionReceipt` returns `null` for a transaction that is in a block.\n- **Cause:** for some days in October 2026 the Ethereum RPC did not serve one transaction's receipt; on Oct 23, 2026 it served it again. Note that a hash the RPC has never seen also answers `null`, with no error, so `null` alone does not tell \"pending\", \"lost\" and \"never sent\" apart.\n- **Fix:** read the result from the node instead: the `revive.ethTransact` extrinsic in the block with the same number, and its events (recipes 5a and 5b, or the chain tool get_transaction).\n- **Not known:** why the receipt was missing.\n- **Basis:** Checked in October 2026 (missing, then served again on Oct 23) and on Nov 14, 2026 (`null` for an unknown hash). The fix is Experience." },
  { id: "E16", title: "Multisig traps", body: "- **You see:** `Multisig.MaxWeightTooLow`, `Multisig.SignatoriesOutOfOrder`, an approval that stored only a hash, a call that \"succeeded\" but did nothing, or approvers whose call hashes differ.\n- **Fix:** each trap, with what to do about it:\n  - Opening with `approveAsMulti` leaves only the call's hash on chain. Open with `asMulti` and the full call.\n  - `otherSignatories` must be sorted by account id. Otherwise the call fails with `SignatoriesOutOfOrder` (\"The signatories were provided out of order; they should be ordered\").\n  - A `maxWeight` that is too low gives `MaxWeightTooLow`. Take the weight from the runtime call `transactionPaymentCallApi.queryCallInfo(call, length)`.\n  - The extrinsic succeeds even when the inner call fails (E9). Read the result inside `MultisigExecuted`.\n  - The opener reserves `depositBase` + `depositFactor` x threshold until the call runs: 5.43 + 1.92 x 2 = 9.27 tPOT for 2 of 3.\n  - Approvers who dry-run at different times get different limits, and so a different call hash. Round the limits so that every approver builds the same call.\n  - What `Multisig.asMulti` answered in dry runs: a threshold of 1, `MinimumThreshold`; the sender among the other signatories, `SenderInSignatories` (also from `asMultiThreshold1`); 101 signatories in all, `TooManySignatories` (the maximum is 100); a timepoint given when opening, `UnexpectedTimepoint`; no timepoint when approving an open operation, `NoTimepoint`; a timepoint that is not the opening one, `WrongTimepoint`; the same signatory approving twice, `AlreadyApproved`. `cancelAsMulti` of an operation that does not exist answered `Multisig.NotFound`. From an account with no balance, `asMulti` answered `Balances.InsufficientBalance`: it cannot reserve the deposit.\n  - In a dry run, a new operation's timepoint is {height: the best block, index: 0}. The approval that matched it (`AlreadyApproved`) answered `WrongTimepoint` when dry-run again at a later block.\n  - Not only multisig: in a dry run, `Utility.batch`, `Utility.forceBatch`, `Proxy.proxy`, `Sudo.sudo` and `Sudo.sudoAs` also answer as if they succeeded when the call inside fails (E29).\n- **Basis:** Experience from multisig rounds on Oct 20, 2026. The error names, their texts and the deposit constants Checked on Nov 14, 2026. The `asMulti` and `cancelAsMulti` replies and the timepoint Checked on Nov 15, 2026 by dry run through the pallets address (an approval was dry-run as `Utility.batchAll` [open, approve]); most of them measured twice, at two blocks. `MaxWeightTooLow` was not measured: it needs a second signatory's approval." },
  { id: "E17", title: "The account is removed, or a transfer is refused near the minimum balance", body: "- **You see:** an account that vanished after a transfer, a transfer refused although the balance seems large enough, or a payment to a new account that fails.\n- **Cause:** the existential deposit, the smallest balance an account may hold, is 0.1 tPOT.\n  - `transferAllowDeath` may take an account below it, which removes the account and loses the rest. `transferKeepAlive` refuses instead.\n  - A payment that creates a new account must be at least 0.1 tPOT.\n  - `eth_getBalance` already leaves the 0.1 out, so a wallet's \"max\" on the Ethereum side keeps it. It shows (free - max(frozen, existential deposit)) x 10000 wei + the account's dust; reserved tPOT does not lower it.\n  - What the calls answered in dry runs: 0.01 tPOT to a new account, `Token(BelowMinimum)`, from both `transferKeepAlive` and `transferAllowDeath`. A `transferKeepAlive` that would leave the sender 0.05 tPOT, `Token(NotExpendable)`; the same with `transferAllowDeath` was accepted. A transfer of 0 to an account that does not exist was accepted, and so was one of 0 from such an account.\n  - An Assets token has its own minimum (`minBalance`): 1 unit of a token whose minimum is 10000, sent to an account that holds none of it, answered `Token(BelowMinimum)`. 10000 units of a token that is not sufficient, sent to an account with no tPOT, answered `Token(CannotCreate)`.\n- **Fix:** use `transferKeepAlive` for everyday sends, keep 0.1 tPOT on every account you use, and send at least 0.1 tPOT to a new account.\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 14, 2026 (the 0.1 tPOT; the `eth_getBalance` rule on 19 accounts of both networks, among them accounts with frozen and with reserved tPOT; no account had both, so that case is not measured). The behaviour of the two transfer calls is Experience from the chain's own text for them. The dry-run replies Checked on Nov 15, 2026 through the pallets address (E29), most of them measured twice; nothing was sent, so what a sent transfer of 0 does is not measured." },
  { id: "E18", title: "Not enough tPOT to deploy (no faucet, large deposits)", body: "- **You see:** `Revive.StorageDepositNotEnoughFunds`, error 1010 or \"insufficient funds for gas * price + value\" when you deploy, and no faucet to top up from.\n- **Cause:** there is no self-serve official faucet. Deposits are large: new runtime code of L bytes holds 0.078 x L + 13.11 tPOT (83.232 tPOT for 899 bytes), and every new storage slot 3.8415 tPOT (E2).\n- **Fix:**\n  - Shrink the code: build without CBOR metadata (`cbor_metadata = false`, `bytecode_hash = \"none\"`), use the IR pipeline, and drop getters you do not need. One contract went from 1842 to 899 bytes this way (recipe 2a).\n  - Avoid new storage slots.\n  - Dry-run the deploy to see the deposit before you ask for tPOT (the chain tool simulate_contract_deploy).\n  - Some community faucets pay only `5...` addresses: give them the fallback `5...` address of your `0x` address (E4).\n- **Others report:** that testnet tPOT is given through a ticket in the Portaldot Discord, in limited amounts; deploys of several contracts that cost 400 to 1386 tPOT; and builders blocked for days while they waited for tokens.\n- **Basis:** Checked: the deposit rule by dry run on Nov 14, 2026, and no official faucet on Nov 13, 2026. The code size work is Experience." },
  { id: "E19", title: "Standard EVM helpers are missing (Multicall3, CREATE2 deployer, Safe, Permit2, EntryPoint)", body: "- **You see:** viem, wagmi or deploy scripts fail on calls to the standard addresses. `eth_getCode` returns `0x` there, and an `eth_call` to them returns `0x` with no error, which libraries report as a call that returned no data.\n- **Cause:** none of these is deployed on the testnet: Multicall3 `0xcA11bde05977b3631167028862bE2a173976CA11`, the CREATE2 proxy `0x4e59b44847b379578588920cA78FbF26c0B4956C`, Permit2 `0x000000000022D473030F116dDEE9F6B43aC78BA3`, EntryPoint v0.7 `0x0000000071727De22E5E9d8BAf0edAc6f37da032`, the Safe singleton factory `0x914d7Fec6aaC8cd542e72Bca78B30650d45643d7` and CreateX `0xba5Ed099633D3B313e4D5F7bdc1305d3c28ba5Ed`, among others. No chain list has an entry for 420420777 either, so wallets and tools do not know the chain.\n- **Fix:**\n  - Add the network by hand: chain id 420420777, RPC `https://test-evm-rpc-v3.portaldot.dev`, symbol tPOT, 18 decimals.\n  - For batched reads, use a deployless `eth_call` (no `to`, creation code as `data`), which works; keep each batch small (E32). Whether viem's deployless multicall works end to end is not tested.\n  - For batched writes, `utility.batchAll` is a native call (recipe 4). `eth_estimateGas` for `utility.batchAll` through the pallets address answered an estimate (Checked Nov 14, 2026: from the Treasury's `0x` form, data `0x01020400071468656c6c6f`, a batch of one `system.remarkWithEvent(\"hello\")`, about 0.0136 tPOT); nothing was sent. That a sent batch works is Reported, not checked.\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 13 and 14, 2026 (`eth_getCode` at these addresses on Nov 14, deployless `eth_call` on both days, no chain-list entry on Nov 13, the `utility.batchAll` estimate on Nov 14). That a sent batch works is Reported." },
  { id: "E20", title: "No EVM explorer and no source verification", body: "- **You see:** no explorer finds the `0x` transaction hash MetaMask shows, and `forge verify` has no target.\n- **Cause:** the official explorers show the Substrate side only, and Sourcify does not list chain 420420777.\n- **Fix:**\n  - Read the receipt through the Ethereum RPC, or use the chain tool get_transaction, which also finds the node's side.\n  - Open the block in the Node Console or the explorer by its block number, which is the same on both sides (recipe 5d).\n  - Publish the code hash (`cast code <address> --rpc-url <rpc> | cast keccak`) so others can compare it with their own build.\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 13, 2026. The fix is Experience." },
  { id: "E21", title: "eth_estimateGas ignores a state override; there is no eth_simulateV1", body: "- **You see:** `eth_estimateGas` with a state override gives the same answer, or the same error, as without it. `eth_simulateV1` answers `{\"code\":-32601,\"message\":\"Method not found\"}`.\n- **Cause:** limits of the Ethereum RPC (client `eth-rpc/main-fddd431`). An estimate from an empty address with a balance override still answered \"insufficient funds\".\n- **Fix:**\n  - `eth_call` does take a state override as its third parameter: the same call from an empty address with a balance override succeeded.\n  - For a \"what if\" estimate, use the runtime call `reviveApi.ethTransactWithConfig(tx, config)`, whose `config` has a `stateOverrides` field.\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 14, 2026 (both overrides, \"Method not found\", and the shape of `ethTransactWithConfig`). The fix with `ethTransactWithConfig` is Experience from Oct 19, 2026." },
  { id: "E22", title: "Windows traps for tools", body: "- **You see:** a `cast` or `forge` command that uses a key or an RPC you did not give it; a `cast` command with large init code that Windows refuses to start; a Node script that aborts at exit while @polkadot/api is still starting; `EPERM`, `EBUSY` or `EACCES` when a script rewrites a JSON file.\n- **Fix:** each trap, with what to do about it:\n  - Foundry loads the `.env` of the folder it runs in and of the project root above it. Run `cast` in an empty directory, without `ETH_*` variables.\n  - Windows refuses a command line longer than about 30000 characters, so big init code does not fit as an argument. Build and sign such a transaction in a script instead.\n  - `process.exit()` while @polkadot/api's WebAssembly crypto is starting can abort the process. Set `process.exitCode` and let the program end.\n  - Writing a file over an old one can fail for a moment while something else reads it. Retry after a short wait.\n- **Basis:** Experience." },
  { id: "E23", title: "Sample code with well-known keys", body: "- **You see:** samples that sign with `//Alice`, `FERDIE` or a mnemonic written into the code, and that print private keys.\n- **Cause:** the guide's Python samples are written that way.\n- **Fix:** never reuse those keys or that phrase, and never print a key. Use a fresh test key that holds testnet tokens only, and load it from outside the code.\n- **Basis:** Guide (its Python samples), read in October 2026." },
  { id: "E24", title: "Other traps reported by other builders", body: "- **Fix:** test each of these on the testnet before you rely on it or design around it. Do not use `block.prevrandao` as randomness.\n- **Others report:**\n  - `block.prevrandao` is the same constant on every block. Checked on Nov 14, 2026: 2500000000000000 at 11 testnet blocks from block 1 to the head, and the same on the mainnet.\n  - Staking rewards nobody claims are lost after 84 eras. Checked on Nov 14, 2026: `staking.historyDepth` is 84, and the chain keeps reward data only for that many eras.\n  - The native Scheduler refuses signed origins with `BadOrigin`. Checked on Nov 15, 2026 by dry run through the pallets address, each once: `Scheduler.scheduleAfter` and `Scheduler.cancel` from a signed account answered `Failed to dispatch call: BadOrigin`, and so did `Preimage.requestPreimage`, `Treasury.spendLocal`, `Treasury.removeApproval`, `Utility.dispatchAs` (with the Root origin), `Utility.withWeight` and `System.setCode`.\n  - Revive has no ed25519, BLS or staking precompile. Checked on Nov 14, 2026 for BLS: the BLS12-381 precompiles (`0x0b` to `0x11`) are absent, and so is KZG (`0x0a`, E31). The precompiles `0x01` to `0x09` (`ecrecover`, `sha256`, `ripemd160`, identity, modexp, the bn256 curve, blake2f) and P256VERIFY at `0x100` work. ed25519 and staking precompiles were not checked.\n  - The Portaldot wallet cannot sign Ethereum transactions, so a second wallet is needed for the Ethereum side.\n- **Basis:** Reported, except the parts marked Checked (by `eth_call` and the node's dry runs; `evm-checks.md` sections 1 and 2; the Scheduler and the other `BadOrigin` replies by dry run on Nov 15, 2026, at testnet blocks 474737 to 474741)." },
  { id: "E25", title: "The Nfts \"Account\" attribute namespace needs approval", body: "- **You see:** setting an attribute in the \"Account\" namespace fails with `Nfts.NoPermission` (\"The signing account has no permission to do the operation\").\n- **Cause:** the upstream code requires the item's owner to approve the account first. The guide does not mention this step.\n- **Fix:** the item's owner calls `Nfts.approveItemAttributes(collection, item, delegate)` first.\n- **Not known:** whether V3 behaves exactly so. We have not tested it on V3.\n- **Basis:** Experience from reading the upstream code in October 2026. The call and the error exist on the chain (Checked on Nov 14, 2026)." },
  { id: "E26", title: "eth_call or eth_estimateGas answers \"execution reverted\"", body: "- **You see:** code 3 with the revert data in `data`:\n  - `{\"code\":3,\"message\":\"execution reverted: revert: <message>\",\"data\":\"0x08c379a0...\"}` for a `require` or `revert` with a message;\n  - `{\"code\":3,\"message\":\"execution reverted: panic: arithmetic underflow or overflow (0x11)\",\"data\":\"0x4e487b71...\"}` for a Solidity panic;\n  - `{\"code\":3,\"message\":\"execution reverted\",\"data\":\"0xdeadbeef...\"}` (no colon) for a custom error;\n  - `{\"code\":3,\"message\":\"execution reverted: \",\"data\":\"0x\"}` for a revert without a reason;\n  - `{\"code\":3,\"message\":\"execution reverted: revert: Metadata not found\",\"data\":\"0x08c379a0...\"}` from `name()`, `symbol()` or `decimals()` at the ERC-20 address of an Assets-module token (`contracts.md`).\n- **Cause:** the contract's code reverted during the dry run. The node keeps the revert data in the `data` field.\n- **Fix:**\n  - Decode `data`: `0x08c379a0` is `Error(string)`, `0x4e487b71` is `Panic(uint256)`, and anything else is a custom error of the contract, which its ABI decodes (`cast 4byte-decode`, viem's `decodeErrorResult`). The chain tool explain_error decodes the first two, and names the common custom errors of OpenZeppelin Contracts 5 by their selectors. Match on `data`, not on the message.\n  - A revert without a reason is often a function selector the contract does not have (and no fallback function), an inner call from the contract to an address with no code (E19), a wrong `msg.sender` (the caller's `0x` form, E4) or a value in the wrong scale (E3). A direct `eth_call` to an address with no code does not revert: it returns `0x`.\n  - \"Metadata not found\" means that asset id has no metadata: no such asset, or a pool token (PoolAssets have none). `totalSupply()` and `balanceOf()` at the same address answer 0 even when the asset does not exist, so check the asset first (`assets.asset(id)`).\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 14, 2026 (all five texts, from deployless `eth_call`, `eth_call` to token addresses, and `eth_estimateGas`; a dry run of the unknown selector `0xdeadbeef` on an ERC-20 reverted with data `0x`)." },
  { id: "E27", title: "\"Invalid call: Payment\" with a small gas limit", body: "- **You see:** `eth_call` or `eth_estimateGas` with a `gas` field answers `{\"code\":-32000,\"message\":\"Invalid call: Payment\"}`.\n- **Cause:** the gas limit is far below what V3 needs, because gas includes the storage deposit (E2). A limit of 21000, the cost of a plain transfer on Ethereum, gave this answer for a 1 tPOT transfer; so did a limit of 1.\n- **Fix:** leave `gas` out of `eth_call` and `eth_estimateGas`. For a transaction, use `eth_estimateGas` + 10% (E1), never a fixed number from Ethereum habits.\n- **Not known:** what `eth_sendRawTransaction` answers for a transaction with such a limit from an account that can pay. On Nov 15, 2026 a new, empty account sent one with a gas limit of 21000: the bare `{\"code\":1010,\"message\":\"Invalid Transaction\"}` came back, and the node's own check of such a transaction said `Payment` (recipe 2f). An empty account fails the balance check anyway, so this does not show a separate check of the gas limit.\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 14, 2026 (gas limits 21000 and 1). The dry runs with these limits Checked again on Nov 15, 2026, and the refused transaction sent that day from a new, empty account." },
  { id: "E28", title: "\"Method not found\" (-32601)", body: "- **You see:** `{\"code\":-32601,\"message\":\"Method not found\"}`.\n- **Cause:** the endpoint does not have that method.\n  - An `eth_` method sent to a Substrate node (`https://test-node-v3.portaldot.dev` or the mainnet node). Ethereum methods live on the separate Ethereum RPC, and the mainnet has none (E13).\n  - A method the Ethereum RPC does not offer, such as `eth_simulateV1` (E21), or a misspelt name.\n- **Fix:** send Ethereum methods to `https://test-evm-rpc-v3.portaldot.dev`, and Substrate methods to the node. To see which Substrate methods a node offers, call `rpc_methods`.\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 14, 2026 (unknown methods and `eth_chainId` on both nodes; `eth_simulateV1` on the Ethereum RPC)." },
  { id: "E29", title: "\"Failed to dispatch call\" when a 0x account estimates a native call", body: "- **You see:** `eth_estimateGas` or `eth_call` to the pallets address `0x6d6f646c70792f70616464720000000000000000` (recipe 4) answers with code -32000 and the reason the native call failed:\n  - `{\"code\":-32000,\"message\":\"Failed to dispatch call: Token(FundsUnavailable)\"}`\n  - `{\"code\":-32000,\"message\":\"Failed to dispatch call: BadOrigin\"}`\n  - `{\"code\":-32000,\"message\":\"Failed to dispatch call: Module(ModuleError { index: 39, error: [3, 0, 0, 0], message: Some(\\\"Unknown\\\") })\"}`\n  - `{\"code\":-32000,\"message\":\"Invalid call: Call\"}`\n- **Cause:** the node ran the native call as a dry run, and the call failed. The text after \"Failed to dispatch call:\" is the call's own error:\n  - `Token(FundsUnavailable)`: the account cannot pay an amount that the call moves. A `Balances.transferKeepAlive` of 0.01 tPOT from an account with no balance gave it.\n  - `BadOrigin`: the call needs another origin, such as root. `Balances.forceTransfer` gave it.\n  - `Module(ModuleError { index, error, message })`: an error of the module with that index, named by `message`. `Assets.transfer` of an asset id that does not exist gave `Assets.Unknown` (module 39, error 3).\n  - `Invalid call: Call`: the transaction is not a native call this route takes. Data that names no module (`0xffff`), data that is missing or `0x`, and a value above 0 (10000 wei and 1 tPOT tried) each gave it. Send such a call with value 0.\n  - The estimate does not check that the account can pay the fee: the same empty account got an estimate for `System.remarkWithEvent`. The node checks that when the transaction is sent (E1).\n  - Measured on Nov 15, 2026 in 143 dry runs of well-formed calls of 18 modules (Balances, Assets, Nfts, Uniques, AssetConversion, Multisig, Proxy, Utility, Staking, NominationPools, Identity, Vesting, Preimage, Scheduler, Sudo, Treasury, System, Revive): every refusal came in this form, with code -32000 and no `data` field, and none was `Invalid call: Call`. A module error carries its name in the text, as `message: Some(\"Name\")`. `eth_call` gave the same text as `eth_estimateGas`.\n  - The call runs as the account the `0x` address stands for. From the `0x` form of a native account that exists (and so is linked to it), it runs as that account: a transfer from the Treasury's `0x` form was judged against the Treasury's own balance, and a validator's `0x` form got `Staking.AlreadyBonded`. From an address that is not linked to a native account, such as the `0x` form of a native account that does not exist yet, it runs as its fallback account (E4).\n  - Some names mislead. `Nfts.mint` into a collection that does not exist answers `Nfts.NoConfig`, while `Nfts.transfer` answers `Nfts.UnknownCollection`. A swap on a pair that has no pool answers `AssetConversion.PoolEmpty`, whose text says \"The pool exists but has no liquidity\". `Assets.cancelApproval` of an approval that does not exist, on an asset that exists, answers `Assets.Unknown` (\"The given asset ID is unknown\"). `AssetConversion.createPool` with an asset that does not exist answers `Assets.Unknown` too: there the name is right, but the error comes from the Assets module (39), not from AssetConversion. `references/native-call-errors.md` lists the replies we measured, call by call.\n  - A dry run of a call that wraps another shows only the outer result. `Utility.batch`, `Utility.forceBatch` and `Proxy.proxy` answered with an estimate, as for success, although the call inside failed with `BadOrigin`; `Utility.batchAll` answered `Failed to dispatch call: BadOrigin`. `Sudo.sudo` of `Balances.transferKeepAlive`, a call that Root cannot make, dry-run with the sudo key's `0x` form as the sender (a dry run may name any sender), answered with an estimate too, and so did `Sudo.sudoAs` of `Balances.forceSetBalance`, a call that a signed account cannot make. An estimate carries no events, so it cannot show the inner results.\n  - From an account with no balance, `Assets.create`, `Assets.touch`, `Nfts.create`, `Multisig.asMulti` and `Identity.setIdentity` answered `Balances.InsufficientBalance` (module 6, error 2), while transfers and `Preimage.notePreimage` answered `Token(FundsUnavailable)`.\n- **Fix:**\n  - Build the data with the chain tool encode_call and check its echo of the arguments. With `sender`, it also checks the balance and dry-runs the call from that sender, so a refusal shows before anything is signed.\n  - Fund the account's fallback `5...` address before you estimate a call that moves tPOT (E4).\n  - Name a module error with the chain tool explain_error, or with `api.registry.findMetaError` (E10).\n  - A call that needs root cannot be sent from a `0x` account.\n  - To see an inner error in a dry run, estimate the inner call alone from the account it runs as (for `Proxy.proxy`, the real account's `0x` form; a dry run may name any sender), or wrap it in `Utility.batchAll`. Neither works for `Sudo.sudo`: its inner call runs as Root, no dry run through the pallets address runs as Root, and a root-only call estimated alone answers `BadOrigin`.\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 14, 2026 (all four texts from both `eth_call` and `eth_estimateGas`; `Invalid call: Call` for a value and for missing data, from both as well; the empty account's estimate for `System.remarkWithEvent`). Whether a sent transaction with a value is refused the same way was not tested. The 143 dry runs Checked on Nov 15, 2026 (`eth_estimateGas`, and `eth_call` once, at testnet blocks 474727 to 474801, most of them measured twice at two blocks; several of the misleading names, `Utility.batch` and the sudo case once more at blocks 474925 to 474964; `Sudo.sudoAs` twice, later that day). Nothing was sent, so these replies are not confirmed against the events of a sent transaction." },
  { id: "E30", title: "eth_subscribe refuses \"newHeads\" on the WebSocket endpoint", body: "- **You see:** a WebSocket client of the Ethereum RPC (`wss://test-evm-rpc-v3.portaldot.dev`) that subscribes to new blocks gets ``{\"code\":-32602,\"message\":\"Invalid params\",\"data\":\"unknown variant `newHeads`, expected `newBlockHeaders` or `logs` at line 1 column 10\"}``. Over HTTPS, `eth_subscribe` answers `{\"code\":-32603,\"message\":\"Internal error\"}`.\n- **Cause:** the Ethereum RPC names its subscriptions `newBlockHeaders` and `logs`. It takes neither the standard `newHeads` nor `newPendingTransactions`, and it has no filters (`eth_newFilter` and `eth_newBlockFilter` answer \"Method not found\", E28).\n- **Fix:**\n  - Subscribe with `[\"newBlockHeaders\"]` (one notification per block) or `[\"logs\", {...}]` on `wss://test-evm-rpc-v3.portaldot.dev`.\n  - A library that subscribes with `newHeads` (for example a block watcher on a WebSocket transport): give it the HTTPS endpoint and let it poll `eth_blockNumber` and `eth_getLogs`.\n- **Not known:** whether a `logs` subscription delivers logs: none was emitted while one was open.\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 14, 2026 (both refusals, `newBlockHeaders` notifications and `eth_unsubscribe`, measured twice; `evm-checks.md` section 5)." },
  { id: "E31", title: "\"failed to run contract\": InvalidInstruction or UnsupportedPrecompileAddress", body: "- **You see:** `eth_call` or `eth_estimateGas` answers with code -32000 and no revert data:\n  - `{\"code\":-32000,\"message\":\"failed to run contract: Module(ModuleError { index: 80, error: [31, 0, 0, 0], message: Some(\\\"InvalidInstruction\\\") })\"}`\n  - `{\"code\":-32000,\"message\":\"failed to run contract: Module(ModuleError { index: 80, error: [47, 0, 0, 0], message: Some(\\\"UnsupportedPrecompileAddress\\\") })\"}`\n  - The node's own dry runs (`reviveApi.call`, `reviveApi.instantiate`) report them as `Revive.InvalidInstruction` (module 80, error 31) and `Revive.UnsupportedPrecompileAddress` (module 80, error 47).\n- **Cause:** the code ran into something this chain's EVM does not have. It is not a revert.\n  - `InvalidInstruction`: the blob opcodes `BLOBHASH` (`0x49`) and `BLOBBASEFEE` (`0x4a`), `INVALID` (`0xfe`) and unassigned opcodes such as `0x0c`. A blob opcode on a path that never runs does no harm. `PUSH0`, `MCOPY`, `TSTORE` and `TLOAD` work.\n  - `UnsupportedPrecompileAddress`: a call straight to `0x000000000000000000000000000000000000000a`, the KZG point evaluation of EIP-4844. A `STATICCALL` to it from contract code returns failure instead.\n  - The BLS12-381 precompiles (`0x0b` to `0x11`) are absent too, but a call to them succeeds with no data.\n- **Fix:** build for `cancun` without blob opcodes, KZG or BLS, and check the length of the data any precompile returns. `evm-checks.md` sections 1 and 2 list what works.\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 14, 2026 (deployless `eth_call` and `eth_call` to `0x0a` and `0x0b` on the testnet, and the node's dry runs on both networks)." },
  { id: "E32", title: "\"The transaction consumes more than the allowed weight\" from eth_call", body: "- **You see:** a heavy `eth_call`, for example a deployless batch of many reads, answers `{\"code\":-32000,\"message\":\"The transaction consumes more than the allowed weight. needed=Weight(ref_time: 1531321961872, proof_size: 23813914) allowed=Weight(ref_time: 1529902658700, proof_size: 14111759216387806985) overweight_by=Weight(ref_time: 1419303172, proof_size: 0)\"}`. A heavier one fails with `failed to run contract: Module(ModuleError { index: 80, error: [3, 0, 0, 0], message: Some(\\\"OutOfGas\\\") })`.\n- **Cause:** a call is bounded by the weight of one extrinsic (about 1.53 x 10^12 ref_time), not by the block gas limit, which reads 2^64 - 1. A deployless call that made 4096 `STATICCALL`s to empty addresses passed; 5120 gave the first text, and 6144 or more `Revive.OutOfGas`.\n- **Fix:** split the work into smaller calls. Real reads cost more weight each than these empty calls, so keep batches far below that size. Do not size work by `block.gaslimit` or `gasleft()`.\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 14, 2026 (deployless `eth_call` with 4096 to 12288 `STATICCALL`s; `evm-checks.md` section 6)." },
  { id: "E33", title: "\"Failed to filter logs\" from eth_getLogs", body: "- **You see:** `eth_getLogs` answers `{\"code\":-32000,\"message\":\"Failed to filter logs\"}`.\n- **Cause:** the block range is not valid: `fromBlock` is after `toBlock`, or `toBlock` is beyond the head. The size of the range is not the cause: `eth_getLogs` has no block-range limit (E6).\n- **Fix:** read the head with `eth_blockNumber` first, keep `toBlock` at or below it (or use `latest`), and keep `fromBlock` at or below `toBlock`. An indexer that runs ahead of the chain meets this when it asks for blocks that do not exist yet.\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 14, 2026 (both cases, measured twice; ranges up to the whole chain answered)." },
  { id: "E34", title: "Errors 1040, 1001 or 1002: the node cannot read the bytes as a transaction", body: "- **You see:** the Substrate node refuses the bytes before it checks anything else. As @polkadot/api 17 prints the replies:\n  - from `author_submitExtrinsic`: ``1040: Client error: Could not decode `OpaqueExtrinsic.0`: Not enough data to decode vector``;\n  - from `author_submitAndWatchExtrinsic`, for the same kind of bytes: ``1001: Extrinsic has invalid format: Could not decode `OpaqueExtrinsic.0`: Not enough data to decode vector``;\n  - from both methods: ``1002: Verification Error: Runtime error: Execution failed: Execution aborted due to trap: wasm trap: wasm `unreachable` instruction executed``, then a WASM backtrace that ends in `TaggedTransactionQueue_validate_transaction`.\n  - In the node's own texts, a line break and a tab come before \"Not enough data\".\n- **Cause:** the bytes are not a transaction this runtime can read.\n  - 1040 and 1001: the length at the start does not fit the bytes. Measured for bytes cut short, for a raw Ethereum transaction (`0x02f8...`) sent to the Substrate node, and, from `author_submitExtrinsic`, for hex with an odd number of digits (`\"0x123\"`).\n  - 1002: the length fits, but what it covers is not a transaction. Measured for an empty transaction (`0x00`; from `author_submitExtrinsic` only, since the same bytes sent to `author_submitAndWatchExtrinsic` right after got 1012), a length followed by random bytes, the `0x` text of a signed transaction turned into bytes once more, a signed transaction without its length prefix, extra bytes inside the length, and a version byte that is neither `0x84` (signed) nor `0x04` (unsigned): `0x83`, `0x85`, `0x45`, `0x05` and `0xff` tried. The bytes `0x00` sent again got 1012 three times, then 1013 (E12).\n  - Extra bytes after a complete transaction, with its length left as it was, were not noticed: both methods answered as for the transaction alone (1010 \"Inability to pay some fees\", from an empty account).\n  - The runtime's own check fails the same way: `state_call` of `TaggedTransactionQueue_validate_transaction` with such bytes answers ``4003: Client error: Execution failed: Execution aborted due to trap: wasm trap: wasm `unreachable` instruction executed``.\n  - `transaction_v1_broadcast` took bytes cut short without an error: it returned an operation id. `transaction_v1_stop` with that id then answered `-32602: Invalid operation id`.\n- **Fix:**\n  - Build the transaction with @polkadot/api connected to the chain (recipe 0), so that it uses the chain's own metadata, and send `tx.toHex()` as it is. A signed transaction starts with its length, then `0x84` (signed, format 4).\n  - Send `0x` hex of whole bytes, and do not turn the hex text into bytes a second time.\n  - Send Ethereum transactions to the Ethereum RPC (`eth_sendRawTransaction`), not to the node (E13).\n  - To check bytes before you send them, ask the runtime's own check directly: `api.rpc.state.call('TaggedTransactionQueue_validate_transaction', '0x02' + hex.slice(2) + at.slice(2), at)`, with `hex` the extrinsic and `at` a recent block hash, and read the answer as the last lines of recipe 2f do. Recipe 2f as it is checks Ethereum transactions only: it wraps its input in `revive.ethTransact`, so for any Substrate bytes it prints `Invalid Call`. A 4003 with a wasm trap means the runtime cannot read the bytes. `{\"Err\":{\"Invalid\":...}}` or `{\"Err\":{\"Unknown\":...}}` names the check that refuses a transaction it can read.\n  - Do not rely on `transaction_v1_broadcast` to report bad bytes. `author_submitExtrinsic` and `author_submitAndWatchExtrinsic` refuse them.\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 15, 2026: refused submissions to the testnet node (blocks 475079 to 475097) of bytes made for the purpose, from random bytes and from transactions signed by new, empty accounts with random keys held only in memory and then changed. Before each, we read the runtime's own check of the same bytes through `state_call`. Each case was sent once to each method, with fresh bytes of the same kind for the second method (for `0x00` the same bytes, since no others exist). Nothing was accepted." },
  { id: "E35", title: "-32000 \"Invalid transaction\" (lower-case t) from eth_sendRawTransaction", body: "- **You see:** `eth_sendRawTransaction` answers `{\"code\":-32000,\"message\":\"Invalid transaction\"}`: code -32000, and \"transaction\" with a lower-case t.\n- **Cause:** the Ethereum RPC could not decode the bytes as an Ethereum transaction that it takes, so it refused them itself. Nothing reached the node's pool. Measured for:\n  - nonsense hex (`0xdeadbeef`) and empty bytes (`0x`);\n  - a signed EIP-1559 transaction with its last 10 bytes cut off;\n  - a Substrate extrinsic, unsigned or signed, sent to the Ethereum RPC;\n  - a legacy (type 0) transaction without a chain id (`v` = 27 or 28, before EIP-155). The node itself does not refuse it for that: its own check of the same transaction went on to the balance check (`Payment`, from an empty account).\n  - It is not E1. The pool's refusal is `{\"code\":1010,\"message\":\"Invalid Transaction\"}`, with code 1010 and a capital T: the RPC decoded the transaction and passed it on, and the node refused it.\n  - Not all bad input gets this text. Hex that is not hex is refused while the parameter is read, with -32602 (E36). A raw transaction without `0x`, and one with an extra `00` byte at the end, were passed on to the node.\n- **Fix:**\n  - Sign for chain id 420420777. A typed transaction (type 2, as in recipe 2c) carries it; a legacy one must use EIP-155.\n  - Send the signed transaction whole, as hex, exactly as the signer returned it (`cast mktx`, `signTransaction` of viem or ethers).\n  - Send a Substrate extrinsic to the node (`author_submitExtrinsic`), not to the Ethereum RPC.\n- **Not known:** what the RPC does with an extra byte after a signed transaction. It passed one on, and the node refused it for the empty account.\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 15, 2026: refused submissions to `eth_sendRawTransaction` at testnet blocks 474651 to 474654, signed by new, empty accounts with random keys held only in memory, each measured twice; nonsense hex and the legacy case once more at blocks 474908 to 474910. The node's check of the legacy transaction read through `state_call` (recipe 2f), for a copy signed the same way by another empty key, and once more when we repeated the measurement." },
  { id: "E36", title: "-32602 \"Invalid params\": a parameter missing or written wrongly", body: "- **You see:** `{\"code\":-32602,\"message\":\"Invalid params\",\"data\":\"...\"}` from the Ethereum RPC, or `-32602: Invalid params: ...` from the Substrate node, with one of these texts:\n  - `No more params`\n  - `OddLength`, `InvalidHexCharacter { c: 'z', index: 0 }`, or from the node `invalid hex character: h, at 0`\n  - `hex string without 0x prefix`\n  - from `eth_feeHistory`: ``invalid type: integer `4`, expected a (both 0x-prefixed or not) hex string or byte array containing between (0; 32] bytes``\n  - Some of these texts end with `at line 1 column N`; `No more params` and `OddLength` never did.\n- **Cause:**\n  - `No more params`: a required parameter is missing. `eth_getBalance` needs its block (there is no default), `eth_getBlockByNumber` its second parameter (`true` or `false`), and the node's `author_submitExtrinsic` its extrinsic. `eth_call` without a block is accepted and runs at the head.\n  - `OddLength`, `InvalidHexCharacter`, `invalid hex character`: the value is not hex. An odd number of digits (`\"0xabc\"`, or `\"hello\"`), or a character that is not a hex digit (`\"0xzzzz\"` as a raw transaction, `\"0xzz\"` as `data`). The bytes were never read as a transaction.\n  - `hex string without 0x prefix`: a block parameter was read as a hex number. An unknown tag such as `\"newest\"` and a decimal number such as `\"100\"` both answer this; the text does not name the tag.\n  - `invalid type: integer`: a JSON number where the RPC wants a hex string. `eth_feeHistory` takes its block count only as hex: `[\"0x4\", \"latest\", [25, 75]]` answers, `[4, \"latest\", [25, 75]]` does not. A block given as the JSON number `100` answers ``invalid type: integer `100`, expected Block identifier following EIP-1898``.\n  - Other texts of this kind: a `5...` address given to `eth_getBalance`, `invalid length 48, expected a (both 0x-prefixed or not) hex string or byte array containing 20 bytes`; `eth_call` with both `input` and `data`, different, `Both \"data\" and \"input\" are set and not equal. Please use \"input\" to pass transaction call data`; `eth_getLogs` with `blockHash` and `fromBlock`, `cannot specify both blockHash and fromBlock/toBlock, choose one or the other`.\n  - The RPC takes some mistakes without a word: an address without `0x`, a raw transaction without `0x` (passed on to the node), and parameters given as a named object instead of an array.\n- **Fix:**\n  - Give every parameter, in order. Numbers, addresses and data as `0x` hex of whole bytes; a block as `latest`, `pending`, `safe`, `finalized`, `earliest` or a `0x` number.\n  - Give the Ethereum RPC `0x` addresses: convert a `5...` address first (recipe 6, or the chain tool convert_address).\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 15, 2026: requests with wrong parameters to `https://test-evm-rpc-v3.portaldot.dev` (testnet blocks 474651 to 474794), most of them measured twice, and to the node's `author_submitExtrinsic` (block 475097)." },
  { id: "E37", title: "\"Ethereum block not found\" or \"Account not found for address\" (-32000)", body: "- **You see:** `{\"code\":-32000,\"message\":\"Ethereum block not found\"}`, or `{\"code\":-32000,\"message\":\"Account not found for address 0x...\"}` from `eth_sendTransaction`.\n- **Cause:**\n  - `Ethereum block not found`: `eth_getBalance` or `eth_call` was given a block hash that the Ethereum RPC does not know, as `{\"blockHash\": ...}` or as a plain 32-byte string. For the same hash, `eth_getBlockByHash` answers `null`. A block number after the head answers `hash not found` instead (E5).\n  - The Ethereum side has its own block hashes. Block 0 is `0x7c05...7001` there and `0xd4f9...` (the genesis hash) on the node; block 389240 is `0xd618...` there and `0xc000...` on the node. We measured the reply with a random hash, not with a Substrate block hash.\n  - `Account not found for address`: `eth_sendTransaction` asks the RPC to sign, and the RPC holds no keys (`eth_accounts` answers `[]`). `eth_sign` and `eth_signTypedData_v4` answer \"Method not found\" (E28).\n- **Fix:**\n  - Take block hashes from the Ethereum side (`eth_getBlockByNumber`, or a receipt's `blockHash`), or give the block number. The chain tool get_block shows both hashes of a block.\n  - Sign in the wallet or in the script, and send with `eth_sendRawTransaction` (recipe 2c).\n- **Basis:** Checked on Nov 15, 2026: `eth_getBalance`, `eth_call` and `eth_getBlockByHash` with a random block hash, each measured twice; `eth_sendTransaction` from a new, empty account, measured twice; the hashes of blocks 0 and 389240 read on both sides." },
];
